Chapter: GopCalc
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Layout
  cites UI chapter Theme

 The calculator, moved out of `apps/guios/CalcApp.codex` when the guios
 shell retired into GopDesk. The arithmetic and the scancode dispatch are
 that chapter's, unchanged in behaviour; what changed is where the state
 lives and who owns the loop.

 State was a MutWheel, which is GuiTimer's mutable metadata buffer and one
 of the chapters retiring. It is a plain 64-byte cell block here, allocated
 once by `desk-run` and passed in, so entering and leaving the pane
 allocates nothing.

 This chapter has NO run loop and does no drawing. It answers with a
 WidgetNode and GopDesk composes that into the chrome's content panel, so
 the sidebar and taskbar lay out around it and this file never needs to
 know the sidebar's width. GopScene and GopFiles each compute that offset
 themselves and their own prose records that the two files "have to agree
 by hand"; a pane that hands back a tree cannot drift that way.

Section: Operators

  op-none : Integer = 0
  op-add : Integer = 1
  op-sub : Integer = 2
  op-mul : Integer = 3
  op-div : Integer = 4

Section: State

 Three cells: the accumulator, the number being typed, and the pending
 operator.

  gcalc-acc : Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-acc (ks) = peek-32 ks 0

  gcalc-cur : Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-cur (ks) = peek-32 ks 8

  gcalc-op : Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-op (ks) = peek-32 ks 16

Section: Operations

 Typing past the width of the display would run the value off the panel
 and, at ten digits, past what the accumulator can hold usefully. The
 digit is dropped rather than wrapping the value.

  gcalc-digit-cap : Integer = 999999999

  gcalc-digit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-digit (ks) (d) =
    let cur = gcalc-cur ks
    in if cur > gcalc-digit-cap then 0
    else poke-32 ks 8 (cur * 10 + d)

 Division by zero answers zero rather than faulting. There is nothing
 behind this desktop to catch a fault, so the calculator must not be the
 thing that takes the machine down.

  gcalc-eval : Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-eval (ks) =
    let acc = gcalc-acc ks
    in let cur = gcalc-cur ks
    in let op = gcalc-op ks
    in let result = if op == op-add then acc + cur
      else if op == op-sub then acc - cur
      else if op == op-mul then acc * cur
      else if op == op-div then (if cur /= 0 then acc / cur else 0)
      else cur
    in let d1 = poke-32 ks 0 result
    in poke-32 ks 8 0

  gcalc-set-op : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-set-op (ks) (new-op) =
    let d = gcalc-eval ks
    in poke-32 ks 16 new-op

  gcalc-equals : Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-equals (ks) =
    let d = gcalc-eval ks
    in poke-32 ks 16 op-none

  gcalc-clear : Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-clear (ks) =
    let d1 = poke-32 ks 0 0
    in let d2 = poke-32 ks 8 0
    in poke-32 ks 16 op-none

Section: Scancode Dispatch

 Set-1 make codes. The answer is whether the key changed anything, so the
 caller repaints on a key that did and does nothing on one that did not:
 a calculator is event-driven and an idle pane should not be painting.

  gcalc-key : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-key (ks) (sc) =
    if sc == 2 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 1 in 1
    else if sc == 3 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 2 in 1
    else if sc == 4 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 3 in 1
    else if sc == 5 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 4 in 1
    else if sc == 6 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 5 in 1
    else if sc == 7 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 6 in 1
    else if sc == 8 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 7 in 1
    else if sc == 9 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 8 in 1
    else if sc == 10 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 9 in 1
    else if sc == 11 then let d = gcalc-digit ks 0 in 1
    else if sc == 12 then let d = gcalc-set-op ks op-sub in 1
    else if sc == 13 then let d = gcalc-set-op ks op-add in 1
    else if sc == 28 then let d = gcalc-equals ks in 1
    else if sc == 53 then let d = gcalc-set-op ks op-div in 1
    else if sc == 55 then let d = gcalc-set-op ks op-mul in 1
    else if sc == 46 then let d = gcalc-clear ks in 1
    else 0

Section: Display

  gcalc-op-sym : Integer -> Text
  gcalc-op-sym (op) =
    if op == op-add then "+"
    else if op == op-sub then "-"
    else if op == op-mul then "*"
    else if op == op-div then "/"
    else ""

 The big line is what you are typing, or the accumulator when you are not.
 The small line under it is the expression so far, so a pending operator
 is visible rather than remembered.

  gcalc-shown : Integer -> Integer
  gcalc-shown (ks) =
    if gcalc-cur ks > 0 then gcalc-cur ks else gcalc-acc ks

  gcalc-expr : Integer -> Text
  gcalc-expr (ks) =
    if gcalc-op ks /= op-none
    then show (gcalc-acc ks) & " " & gcalc-op-sym (gcalc-op ks) & " " & show (gcalc-cur ks)
    else "= " & show (gcalc-acc ks)

Section: Widget Tree

 The keypad is drawn rather than merely labelled, because the pane is
 reachable by a click and a user who arrived that way has no reason to
 know which keys work. The buttons are not hit-tested yet; the labels are
 the legend for the keyboard.

 The flex is not decoration. A panel is born flexible but a BUTTON is
 not, so inside a DirRow -- where the main axis is width -- a button with
 flex 0 and a min width of 0 lays out exactly 0 pixels wide and paints
 nothing at all. The first capture of this pane showed an empty keypad
 between two separators, with the tree laid out correctly around it.

  gcalc-btn : Text -> WidgetNode
  gcalc-btn (label) = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-button ("gcalc-" & label) label) 0 34) 1

  gcalc-row : Text, Text, Text, Text -> WidgetNode
  gcalc-row (a) (b) (c) (d) =
    widget-panel ("gcalc-r-" & a) DirRow 6 [gcalc-btn a, gcalc-btn b, gcalc-btn c, gcalc-btn d]

  gcalc-tree : Integer -> WidgetNode
  gcalc-tree (ks) =
    let display = widget-panel "gcalc-disp" DirColumn 2 [
      widget-set-min (widget-label "gcalc-val" (show (gcalc-shown ks))) 0 28,
      widget-label "gcalc-expr" (gcalc-expr ks)
    ]
    in let keypad = widget-panel "gcalc-pad" DirColumn 6 [
      gcalc-row "7" "8" "9" "/",
      gcalc-row "4" "5" "6" "*",
      gcalc-row "1" "2" "3" "-",
      widget-panel "gcalc-r-0" DirRow 6 [gcalc-btn "0", gcalc-btn "=", gcalc-btn "+"]
    ]
    in let help = widget-label "gcalc-help" "0-9  + -  * /  Enter equals  C clears  Esc returns"

 Display and keypad are both left flexible and split the pane's height
 between them. Pinning them with `widget-set-flex ... 0` to pull the
 keypad up under the display was tried and is wrong: flex 0 means no
 share of the main axis, and with no minimum height both panels collapsed
 to nothing and every row of the pane drew on top of every other. A
 minimum height per panel is what that would need, and the slack is not
 worth guessing at the layout engine for.

    in widget-panel "gcalc-root" DirColumn 8 [
      display,
      widget-separator "gcalc-sep",
      keypad,
      widget-separator "gcalc-sep2",
      help
    ]
